  "self_critique": "Overall\n\nThe run found a small improvement: 0.603082 versus 0.6018, about +0.00128 absolute. That is useful, but the journal does not establish why it improved, and \u201cconverged\u201d is premature after only three child experiments.\n\nWhat the harness/policy did suboptimally\n\n- It accepted node_003 even though its stated prediction was not met. The hypothesis promised at least +0.002; the observed gain was about +0.00128. Objective acceptance and hypothesis validation should be recorded separately.\n- Node_003 bundled two changes\u2014recency weighting and a multi-seed rank ensemble\u2014so the gain is not attributable. Node_002 already suggests the ensemble alone may provide roughly +0.0008, leaving uncertain incremental value from recency weighting.\n- Several drafts were \u201ckitchen sink\u201d proposals. Node_001 combined architecture, BPR hybridization, dropout, AdamW, decay, and recency weighting. A rejection yields little actionable information.\n- Claims were stronger than the evidence. \u201cTemporal-shift-driven overfit\u201d was inferred merely from validation peaking and declining; ordinary model overfitting is an equally plausible explanation.\n- The initialization-variance claim in node_002 is hard to reconcile with the reported baseline sigma of 0.0001 unless that sigma measures something else. Uncertainty definitions and paired-seed comparisons were not made explicit.\n- Acceptance behavior is opaque. Node_002 improved the rounded primary from 0.6018 to 0.6026 but was rejected, while node_003 was accepted at 0.6031. The journal should report the exact threshold, uncertainty, and statistical comparison behind each decision.\n- Stopping as \u201cconverged\u201d after one accepted branch and no ablations leaves obvious local experiments unexplored.\n\nWhat I would change in the scaffold\n\n- Require atomic experiments by default: one substantive intervention per node.\n- Separate:\n  1. objective improvement,\n  2. statistical confidence,\n  3. whether the numerical hypothesis was confirmed.\n- Record exact scores, seed-level scores, paired deltas, ensemble size, and the meaning of sigma.\n- Require causal ablations before attributing gains from bundled changes.\n- Calibrate predicted effect sizes from prior results rather than repeatedly claiming \u22650.002 gains in a regime where observed improvements are around 0.0005\u20130.0013.\n- Use a convergence rule based on exhausted high-value ablations or lack of improvement across a defined budget, not simply a short sequence of rejections.\n- Guard against validation overfitting from selecting epochs, seeds, ensemble size, and recency hyperparameters on the same split. Prefer a fixed protocol or nested/secondary validation slice.\n\nWhat the next run should try first\n\nA paired ablation of node_003:\n\n- Same FM configuration and exact same consecutive seeds.\n- Compare unweighted versus seven-day recency-weighted training.\n- Evaluate both single-model and fixed-size rank-ensemble outputs.\n\nThis 2\u00d72 comparison will isolate the gains from recency weighting, ensembling, and their interaction. If recency weighting has a reproducible positive paired delta, then sweep a small preregistered half-life set such as 3, 7, 14, and 30 days. If it does not, retain the ensemble-only result and investigate cheaper ensemble sizes, since node_002 indicates that variance reduction may account for much of the winning score."
